/// "About this tool" copy shown below each module's workspace: an intro and a short FAQ.
/// Written for readers arriving from search, so each intro names the concept plainly.
export const moduleAboutEn = {
  aboutHeading: "About this tool",
  aboutFaqHeading: "Frequently asked questions",
  aboutRelatedHeading: "Learn the theory",

  aboutPayoffIntro: "An option payoff diagram shows what a position is worth at expiry for every possible price of the underlying. This strategy builder lets you combine calls, puts, stock, forwards and cash, then plots the payoff and the profit after premiums. Start from presets such as covered calls, protective puts, bull and bear spreads, straddles, strangles and butterflies, or build your own multi-leg strategy.",
  aboutPayoffQ1: "What is the difference between payoff and profit?",
  aboutPayoffA1: "Payoff is the value of the position at expiry. Profit subtracts what you paid to open it (option premiums) and adds what you received for options you sold, so the profit line is the payoff line shifted by the net premium.",
  aboutPayoffQ2: "How can two different strategies have the same payoff?",
  aboutPayoffA2: "Put–call parity: a long call plus a short put with the same strike behaves exactly like a forward. Combinations with identical payoffs must cost the same today, otherwise there would be an arbitrage.",

  aboutBinomialIntro: "The binomial model prices an option by letting the underlying move up or down by fixed factors over discrete steps. Working backwards from the payoffs at expiry with risk-neutral probabilities gives the option value at every node. This interactive tree also shows the replicating portfolio of stock and bond that reproduces the option at each step.",
  aboutBinomialQ1: "What is the risk-neutral probability?",
  aboutBinomialA1: "It is the probability q = (1 + r − d) / (u − d) under which the expected return of the stock equals the risk-free rate. It is not a forecast; it is the weight that makes discounted prices consistent with no arbitrage.",
  aboutBinomialQ2: "How does the binomial tree relate to Black–Scholes?",
  aboutBinomialA2: "As the number of steps grows and the up and down factors are chosen to match the volatility, the binomial price of a European option converges to the Black–Scholes price.",

  aboutBlackScholesIntro: "The Black–Scholes formula gives the price of a European call or put from the stock price, strike, time to maturity, interest rate and volatility. This visualizer plots the price and the Greeks — delta, gamma, vega and theta — across stock prices and maturities, in 2D curves and a 3D surface, so you can see how sensitivities change as an option moves in or out of the money.",
  aboutBlackScholesQ1: "What do the option Greeks measure?",
  aboutBlackScholesA1: "Delta is the price change per unit move in the stock, gamma is how fast delta changes, vega is the sensitivity to volatility and theta is the value lost as time passes, all else equal.",
  aboutBlackScholesQ2: "Why is gamma largest near the strike?",
  aboutBlackScholesA2: "Close to expiry an at-the-money option is about to become either worthless or a share-like payoff, so its delta swings fastest there. Far in or out of the money, delta is already near 1 or 0 and barely moves.",

  aboutHestonIntro: "The Heston model lets volatility itself be random: the variance follows a mean-reverting square-root process that is correlated with the stock price. This simulator shows sample paths of price and variance, the effect of mean reversion speed, long-run variance, vol-of-vol and correlation, and prices a European option by Monte Carlo.",
  aboutHestonQ1: "Why use stochastic volatility instead of Black–Scholes?",
  aboutHestonA1: "Market option prices imply different volatilities for different strikes (the volatility smile and skew). A random, correlated volatility produces fatter tails and skew, which a single constant volatility cannot.",
  aboutHestonQ2: "What does the correlation parameter ρ do?",
  aboutHestonA2: "A negative ρ makes volatility rise when the price falls, the typical equity pattern. It skews the return distribution to the left and steepens the implied volatility skew.",

  aboutDeltaHedgingIntro: "Delta hedging replicates an option by holding delta shares of the stock and a cash position, rebalanced as the price moves. This simulator follows a call option along a simulated stock path and compares the hedging portfolio with the option value, showing the hedge error that remains when you can only rebalance at discrete times.",
  aboutDeltaHedgingQ1: "Why does a delta hedge not work perfectly?",
  aboutDeltaHedgingA1: "Delta changes continuously, but a real hedge is only adjusted at discrete times. Between rebalances the option's curvature (gamma) creates a gain or loss that the linear stock position cannot match.",
  aboutDeltaHedgingQ2: "What happens if I rebalance more often?",
  aboutDeltaHedgingA2: "The hedge error shrinks. In the Black–Scholes model, with continuous rebalancing it would vanish, which is exactly the argument that gives the Black–Scholes price.",

  aboutBondPricingIntro: "A bond's price is the present value of its coupons and principal, discounted at the yield. This bond pricing workbench lays out the cash-flow timeline, discounts each payment, and connects the price to duration and convexity on a draggable price–yield curve.",
  aboutBondPricingQ1: "Why do bond prices fall when yields rise?",
  aboutBondPricingA1: "The cash flows are fixed, so a higher discount rate makes each of them worth less today. The longer the payments are in the future, the stronger the effect.",
  aboutBondPricingQ2: "What is the difference between duration and convexity?",
  aboutBondPricingA2: "Duration is the slope of the price–yield curve: the approximate percentage price change for a small yield change. Convexity measures the curvature that the straight-line duration estimate misses.",
  aboutBondPricingQ3: "How do you calculate a bond's price?",
  aboutBondPricingA3: "Discount each cash flow at the yield and add them up: price = Σ C / (1 + y)^t + F / (1 + y)^T. For example, a 3-year bond with face value 100, a 5% annual coupon and a 4% yield is worth 5/1.04 + 5/1.04² + 105/1.04³ ≈ 102.78.",
  aboutBondPricingQ4: "What is the difference between the coupon rate and the yield to maturity?",
  aboutBondPricingA4: "The coupon rate fixes the cash payments as a percentage of face value. The yield to maturity is the single discount rate that makes the present value of all remaining payments equal the market price. A bond trading below par has a yield above its coupon rate; a bond above par has a yield below it.",

  aboutYieldCurveIntro: "The yield curve shows interest rates across maturities. In this tool you shape a par yield curve with level, slope and curvature factors, bootstrap the zero-coupon spot curve from it, and derive the forward rates the curve implies between future dates.",
  aboutYieldCurveQ1: "What is bootstrapping a yield curve?",
  aboutYieldCurveA1: "Bootstrapping extracts zero-coupon (spot) rates one maturity at a time: each par bond's price pins down the next spot rate, given the spot rates already found for its earlier coupons.",
  aboutYieldCurveQ2: "What does an inverted yield curve mean?",
  aboutYieldCurveA2: "Short rates are above long rates. The curve then implies falling forward rates, which markets often read as an expectation of lower future rates.",
  aboutYieldCurveQ3: "What is the difference between spot rates and forward rates?",
  aboutYieldCurveA3: "A spot rate is today's yield on a zero-coupon bond maturing at one date. A forward rate is the rate fixed today for borrowing between two future dates, implied by the spot curve through (1 + s₂)² = (1 + s₁)(1 + f₁,₂). With a 1-year spot rate of 3% and a 2-year spot rate of 4%, the one-year rate one year ahead is about 5.0%.",
  aboutYieldCurveQ4: "What are the level, slope and curvature of the yield curve?",
  aboutYieldCurveA4: "They are the three typical ways the curve moves. Level shifts all rates up or down together, slope changes the gap between long and short rates (steepening or flattening), and curvature bends the middle maturities relative to both ends. Together they explain most day-to-day moves of government yield curves.",

  aboutDurationConvexityIntro: "Duration and convexity approximate how a bond's price reacts to yield changes. This tool compares the exact price–yield curve with the first-order duration line and the second-order duration-plus-convexity parabola, so you can see how large a yield shock each approximation can handle.",
  aboutDurationConvexityQ1: "When is duration alone good enough?",
  aboutDurationConvexityA1: "For small yield moves. As the move grows, the curvature of the price–yield relationship makes the straight duration line underestimate the price, and the convexity term becomes significant.",
  aboutDurationConvexityQ2: "Why is positive convexity valuable?",
  aboutDurationConvexityA2: "With positive convexity the price rises more when yields fall than it drops when yields rise by the same amount, so for equal duration the more convex bond performs better under large rate moves.",
  aboutDurationConvexityQ3: "What is the difference between Macaulay and modified duration?",
  aboutDurationConvexityA3: "Macaulay duration is the present-value-weighted average time until the cash flows arrive, in years. Modified duration divides it by (1 + y/k), where k is the number of coupon periods per year, and gives the approximate percentage price change for a one-percentage-point change in yield. A modified duration of 7 means a 1% rise in yield lowers the price by about 7%.",
  aboutDurationConvexityQ4: "How do you estimate a bond's price change with duration and convexity?",
  aboutDurationConvexityA4: "ΔP / P ≈ −D_mod × Δy + ½ × C × (Δy)². For a bond with modified duration 7 and convexity 60, a one-percentage-point rise in yield (Δy = 0.01) gives about −7% + 0.3% = −6.7%. The convexity term adds to the estimate whichever way yields move.",

  aboutTreasuryFuturesIntro: "Treasury bond futures can be settled by delivering any bond from a basket. Conversion factors put the bonds on a common footing, the invoice price determines what the buyer pays, and the seller delivers the cheapest-to-deliver bond. This lab shows conversion factors, invoice prices and deliverable cash flows, and how the cheapest-to-deliver bond switches as yields move.",
  aboutTreasuryFuturesQ1: "What is the cheapest-to-deliver bond?",
  aboutTreasuryFuturesA1: "The deliverable bond that is cheapest for the futures seller: the one with the lowest cost of buying it relative to the invoice amount received, i.e. the highest implied repo return.",
  aboutTreasuryFuturesQ2: "Why does the cheapest-to-deliver bond change with yields?",
  aboutTreasuryFuturesA2: "Conversion factors are computed at a fixed notional yield. When market yields move away from it, bonds with different durations gain or lose relative value, so a different bond becomes cheapest.",
  aboutTreasuryFuturesQ3: "What is a conversion factor in Treasury futures?",
  aboutTreasuryFuturesA3: "The price per unit of face value at which a deliverable bond would trade if its yield equalled the contract's notional coupon (6% for CBOT Treasury futures). Multiplying the futures price by it adjusts for bonds with higher coupons or longer maturities being worth more than the standard bond.",
  aboutTreasuryFuturesQ4: "How is the invoice price of a Treasury futures delivery calculated?",
  aboutTreasuryFuturesA4: "Invoice amount = futures settlement price × conversion factor × face value + accrued interest on the delivered bond. The seller receives this amount and delivers the bond, so the cheapest bond to deliver is the one whose market price is lowest relative to its invoice amount.",
  aboutInterestRateSwapIntro: "An interest rate swap exchanges fixed-rate payments for floating-rate payments on the same notional. This lab prices a plain-vanilla swap on a curve you can edit: the par swap rate that makes it worth zero, every payment on both legs, how the swap gains or loses value when the curve moves after the trade, and its sensitivity to each part of the curve.",
  aboutInterestRateSwapQ1: "What is the par swap rate?",
  aboutInterestRateSwapA1: "The fixed rate at which the present value of the fixed leg equals that of the floating leg, so the swap is worth zero when it is traded. On a single curve it equals (1 − P(T)) divided by the annuity, the sum of discount factors times the accrual periods.",
  aboutInterestRateSwapQ2: "Why does a payer swap gain when rates rise?",
  aboutInterestRateSwapA2: "The payer pays a fixed rate and receives floating. When the curve rises, expected floating payments rise while the fixed payments stay the same, so the payer receives more than was priced in. A payer swap behaves like being short a fixed-rate bond.",
  aboutInterestRateSwapQ3: "What is DV01 for a swap?",
  aboutInterestRateSwapA3: "The change in the swap's value when rates move by one basis point. The lab bumps every curve quote by 1 bp and revalues the swap; key-rate DV01 bumps one quote at a time to show which maturities drive the risk.",
  aboutInterestRateSwapQ4: "How is a swap like a bond portfolio?",
  aboutInterestRateSwapA4: "Receiving fixed is like holding a fixed-coupon bond financed by issuing a floating-rate note. The floating-rate note is worth par at each reset, so the swap's value moves like the fixed bond's price, with the same sign of duration.",
  aboutCallableBondIntro: "A callable bond lets the issuer redeem it early at a fixed call price. Investors are short that option, so when rates fall the price stops near the call price instead of rising with the straight bond. This lab prices the call, shows where convexity turns negative, compares yield to maturity with yield to worst, and marks the dates on which the issuer would call.",
  aboutCallableBondQ1: "What is negative convexity?",
  aboutCallableBondA1: "The price–yield curve bends the wrong way: as yields fall the price rises ever more slowly because a call becomes likelier, so a fall in yield helps less than a rise hurts. The lab measures it as effective convexity, from prices at ±25 bp.",
  aboutCallableBondQ2: "What is yield to worst?",
  aboutCallableBondA2: "The lowest yield an investor can earn over maturity and every possible call date. For a bond trading above the call price it is usually the yield to the first call, which is why yield to maturity overstates the return.",
  aboutCallableBondQ3: "When does the issuer call?",
  aboutCallableBondA3: "When refinancing is cheaper than the coupons: if the remaining bond is worth more than the call price at the current yield, the issuer gains by redeeming. The timeline marks each call date this way and highlights the first one.",
  aboutCallableBondQ4: "What is effective duration?",
  aboutCallableBondA4: "Duration measured from the actual price change when the whole yield curve moves by a small amount, here 25 bp each way. It is needed because the cash flows of a callable bond change with yields, so the usual duration formula does not apply.",

  aboutRiskReturnIntro: "Combining a risky portfolio with a risk-free asset traces the capital allocation line. This tool shows how expected return and volatility change as you shift weight between the two, how leverage extends the line, and how the Sharpe ratio — excess return per unit of risk — sets its slope.",
  aboutRiskReturnQ1: "What is the Sharpe ratio?",
  aboutRiskReturnA1: "Expected return minus the risk-free rate, divided by volatility. It is the slope of the capital allocation line and the same for every mix of the risky asset with cash.",
  aboutRiskReturnQ2: "What does leverage do on the capital allocation line?",
  aboutRiskReturnA2: "Borrowing at the risk-free rate to hold more than 100% in the risky asset moves you further along the same line: higher expected return and proportionally higher volatility.",
  aboutRiskReturnQ3: "What is a good Sharpe ratio?",
  aboutRiskReturnA3: "It depends on the horizon and the asset class, so compare like with like. As a rough guide for annual figures, broad stock market indices have historically had Sharpe ratios of about 0.3–0.5, a value above 1 is considered very good, and a persistently much higher number is a reason to look for hidden risks.",
  aboutRiskReturnQ4: "How do you calculate the return and risk of a portfolio with a risk-free asset?",
  aboutRiskReturnA4: "With weight w in the risky portfolio and 1 − w in the risk-free asset, the expected return is r_f + w (E[R] − r_f) and the volatility is w σ, because the risk-free asset has no variance. For example, 60% in a portfolio with an 8% expected return and 20% volatility, with r_f = 2%, gives a 5.6% expected return and 12% volatility.",

  aboutCapmIntro: "The Capital Asset Pricing Model links an asset's expected return to its beta, its sensitivity to the market. This tool plots the security market line from the risk-free rate and the market risk premium, places assets by beta, and shows Jensen's alpha as the gap between an asset's expected return and the CAPM prediction.",
  aboutCapmQ1: "How is expected return calculated with CAPM?",
  aboutCapmA1: "E[R] = r_f + β (E[R_m] − r_f): the risk-free rate plus beta times the market risk premium.",
  aboutCapmQ2: "What does a positive alpha mean?",
  aboutCapmA2: "The asset's expected return lies above the security market line, more than CAPM requires for its beta, so it plots as underpriced relative to the model.",
  aboutCapmQ3: "What is beta in finance?",
  aboutCapmA3: "Beta measures how strongly an asset's return moves with the market: β = Cov(R_i, R_m) / Var(R_m). A beta of 1 moves with the market on average, 1.5 moves about 50% more and a beta below 1 is less sensitive. In CAPM only this market risk earns a premium, because firm-specific risk can be diversified away.",
  aboutCapmQ4: "What is the security market line?",
  aboutCapmA4: "The line E[R] = r_f + β (E[R_m] − r_f) that plots required return against beta. It starts at the risk-free rate at β = 0, passes through the market portfolio at β = 1 and has the market risk premium as its slope. Assets above the line have a positive alpha, those below it a negative one.",

  aboutFamaFrenchIntro: "The Fama–French model explains returns with more than the market. Besides market beta, it adds size (small stocks versus big), value (cheap stocks versus expensive), profitability, investment style and, in the Carhart extension, momentum. This lab uses the real monthly factor returns from the Kenneth French Data Library. You can decompose a mystery fund by dragging its loadings, watch a CAPM alpha shrink as factors are added, build the factors yourself from a sort, and test which model prices the 25 size and value portfolios.",
  aboutFamaFrenchQ1: "What are the Fama–French factors?",
  aboutFamaFrenchA1: "The three-factor model uses the market excess return (Mkt-RF), size (SMB, small minus big) and value (HML, high book-to-market minus low). The five-factor model adds profitability (RMW, robust minus weak) and investment (CMA, conservative minus aggressive). Each factor is the return of a long-short portfolio, so it is a return you could in principle earn by trading, not a statistic. Momentum (winners minus losers) is a separate factor added in the Carhart four-factor model.",
  aboutFamaFrenchQ2: "How do I read a factor loading?",
  aboutFamaFrenchA2: "A loading is a regression coefficient, the same idea as beta. A size loading of 0.7 means the fund moves like 0.7 units of the small-minus-big portfolio on top of its market exposure. A positive value loading marks a value tilt, a negative one a growth tilt. The standard error and t-statistic tell you whether the estimate is distinguishable from zero, because a noisy fund can show a large loading that is only chance.",
  aboutFamaFrenchQ3: "Why does a CAPM alpha shrink when factors are added?",
  aboutFamaFrenchA3: "CAPM alpha is whatever the market cannot explain. A fund that holds small value stocks earns the size and value premia, and a single-factor regression books that as alpha. Once the size and value factors enter the regression, their returns are credited to exposures instead. What remains is the part no listed factor explains, which for most funds is small and statistically hard to tell from zero.",
  aboutFamaFrenchQ4: "Where does the data come from?",
  aboutFamaFrenchA4: "The factor returns and the 25 size and book-to-market portfolios come from the Kenneth R. French Data Library, used with attribution. The page ships a snapshot of the monthly value-weighted series from July 1963, so no download is needed while you explore, and the retrieval date is shown at the bottom of the lab. The mystery funds are simulated on top of the real factors with hidden loadings, so your answer can be checked exactly.",
  aboutPortfolioLabIntro: "Diversification works because assets do not move perfectly together. In this two-asset portfolio lab you set expected returns, volatilities and the correlation, and watch the frontier of possible portfolios bend: the lower the correlation, the more risk you can remove, down to the minimum-variance portfolio.",
  aboutPortfolioLabQ1: "Why does lower correlation reduce portfolio risk?",
  aboutPortfolioLabA1: "Portfolio variance includes a covariance term. When correlation is below 1, losses in one asset are partly offset by the other, so portfolio volatility is less than the weighted average of the two volatilities.",
  aboutPortfolioLabQ2: "What is the minimum-variance portfolio?",
  aboutPortfolioLabA2: "The mix of the two assets with the lowest possible volatility. With perfectly negative correlation it can even be risk-free.",
  aboutPortfolioLabQ3: "How do you calculate the volatility of a two-asset portfolio?",
  aboutPortfolioLabA3: "σ_p² = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2 w₁ w₂ ρ σ₁ σ₂, and σ_p is its square root. For example, 50/50 in assets with 20% and 30% volatility and a correlation of 0.2 gives σ_p² = 0.01 + 0.0225 + 0.006 = 0.0385, so σ_p ≈ 19.6%: below the 25% weighted average and even below the less volatile asset.",
  aboutPortfolioLabQ4: "Can diversification eliminate all risk?",
  aboutPortfolioLabA4: "Only in special cases, such as two assets with a correlation of −1. In a large portfolio the firm-specific risk averages out, but the risk common to all assets, market or systematic risk, remains however many assets you hold.",

  aboutEfficientFrontierIntro: "Markowitz portfolio optimization finds the portfolios with the highest expected return for each level of risk: the efficient frontier. This tool solves for the global minimum-variance portfolio, the maximum-Sharpe tangency portfolio and portfolios with a target return or volatility, and shows how weight constraints such as no short selling cost efficiency.",
  aboutEfficientFrontierQ1: "What is the tangency portfolio?",
  aboutEfficientFrontierA1: "The frontier portfolio with the highest Sharpe ratio. The line from the risk-free rate to it is the steepest possible capital allocation line.",
  aboutEfficientFrontierQ2: "Why do constraints matter?",
  aboutEfficientFrontierA2: "Limits such as no short selling shrink the set of allowed portfolios, so the constrained frontier lies on or below the unconstrained one. The gap is the cost of the constraint.",
  aboutEfficientFrontierQ3: "What is the efficient frontier?",
  aboutEfficientFrontierA3: "The set of portfolios with the highest expected return for each level of volatility, or equivalently the lowest volatility for each expected return. It is the upper edge of the region of all possible portfolios, starting at the global minimum-variance portfolio. A portfolio below it is inefficient, because another one offers more return for the same risk.",
  aboutEfficientFrontierQ4: "Why do optimized portfolio weights change so much with the inputs?",
  aboutEfficientFrontierA4: "Mean–variance optimization needs the expected return, volatility and correlations of every asset. Estimated expected returns are noisy and the optimizer leans heavily on whichever assets look best, so small changes in the inputs can move the weights a lot. Constraints such as no short selling or position limits make the weights more stable.",

  aboutNpvIrrIntro: "Net present value and internal rate of return are the core capital budgeting tools. Enter a project's cash flows to see its NPV at a chosen discount rate, its IRR, the profitability index and the NPV profile that shows how value changes as the discount rate moves.",
  aboutNpvIrrQ1: "What is the difference between NPV and IRR?",
  aboutNpvIrrA1: "NPV is the value a project adds today at a given discount rate. IRR is the discount rate at which NPV is zero. Accept a project when NPV is positive, or equivalently, for conventional cash flows, when IRR exceeds the cost of capital.",
  aboutNpvIrrQ2: "When can NPV and IRR disagree?",
  aboutNpvIrrA2: "When ranking mutually exclusive projects of different size or timing, or when cash flows change sign more than once, which can give multiple IRRs. NPV is the more reliable criterion.",
  aboutNpvIrrQ3: "How do you calculate NPV?",
  aboutNpvIrrA3: "Discount each cash flow to today and add them up: NPV = Σ CF_t / (1 + r)^t, with the initial investment as a negative CF₀. For example, investing 1,000 now for 600 in each of the next two years at a 10% discount rate gives NPV = −1,000 + 545.45 + 495.87 ≈ 41.32, so the project adds value.",
  aboutNpvIrrQ4: "What is the profitability index?",
  aboutNpvIrrA4: "The present value of the future cash flows divided by the initial investment. A value above 1 means a positive NPV. It helps rank projects when capital is limited, because it shows the value created per unit invested: future cash flows worth 1,041 today on a 1,000 investment give an index of about 1.04.",

  aboutValuationIntro: "A discounted cash flow (DCF) valuation values a company as the present value of its future free cash flows. This model projects free cash flow, adds a terminal value for the years beyond the forecast, discounts everything at the WACC and arrives at enterprise value.",
  aboutValuationQ1: "What is terminal value in a DCF?",
  aboutValuationA1: "The value of all cash flows after the explicit forecast period, usually from a growing perpetuity: FCF × (1 + g) / (WACC − g). It often makes up most of the total value.",
  aboutValuationQ2: "Why is a DCF so sensitive to WACC and growth?",
  aboutValuationA2: "The terminal value divides by WACC − g. When the two are close, small changes in either assumption change the valuation a lot.",
  aboutValuationQ3: "What is free cash flow to the firm?",
  aboutValuationA3: "The cash a business generates after taxes and after the investment it needs to keep operating and growing, available to shareholders and lenders alike: FCFF = EBIT × (1 − tax rate) + depreciation − capital expenditure − increase in working capital. Because it belongs to all capital providers, it is discounted at the WACC.",
  aboutValuationQ4: "What is the difference between enterprise value and equity value?",
  aboutValuationA4: "Enterprise value is the value of the operating business to all capital providers, which is what a DCF of free cash flow to the firm gives. Equity value is the part that belongs to shareholders: enterprise value minus net debt (debt less cash). Dividing equity value by the number of shares gives a value per share.",

  aboutTvmIntro: "The time value of money says a unit of money today is worth more than the same amount later, because it can earn interest. This compound interest calculator grows a starting principal and regular contributions over time and splits the final balance into the money you paid in and the interest it earned.",
  aboutTvmQ1: "Why does compound interest grow faster over time?",
  aboutTvmA1: "Interest is earned on previously earned interest as well as on the principal, so the balance grows exponentially rather than linearly.",
  aboutTvmQ2: "What is the difference between present and future value?",
  aboutTvmA2: "Future value compounds an amount forward at the interest rate; present value discounts a future amount back to today. They are the same calculation in opposite directions.",
  aboutTvmQ3: "How do you calculate compound interest?",
  aboutTvmA3: "Future value = P × (1 + r/n)^(n·t), where P is the principal, r the annual rate, n the number of compounding periods per year and t the number of years. For example, 1,000 at 5% compounded annually grows to 1,000 × 1.05¹⁰ ≈ 1,628.89 after 10 years.",
  aboutTvmQ4: "What is the rule of 72?",
  aboutTvmA4: "A quick estimate of the doubling time: divide 72 by the interest rate in percent. At 6% a year, money doubles in about 72 / 6 = 12 years; the exact answer is 11.9. The rule works well for rates between about 4% and 12%.",

  aboutWaccIntro: "The weighted average cost of capital is the blended return a company must earn for its equity and debt holders. This WACC calculator combines the cost of equity and the after-tax cost of debt by their market-value weights, and shows how the interest tax shield and leverage move the result.",
  aboutWaccQ1: "Why is the cost of debt taken after tax?",
  aboutWaccA1: "Interest payments are tax-deductible, so the effective cost of debt to the firm is r_d × (1 − tax rate).",
  aboutWaccQ2: "What is WACC used for?",
  aboutWaccA2: "It is the discount rate for a company's free cash flows in a DCF valuation and the hurdle rate for projects with the same risk as the firm.",
  aboutWaccQ3: "How do you calculate WACC with taxes?",
  aboutWaccA3: "WACC = E/V × r_e + D/V × r_d × (1 − T), where E and D are the market values of equity and debt, V = E + D and T is the corporate tax rate. For example, 60% equity at 10%, 40% debt at 5% and a 25% tax rate give 0.6 × 10% + 0.4 × 5% × 0.75 = 7.5%.",
  aboutWaccQ4: "How is the cost of equity estimated?",
  aboutWaccA4: "Most often with CAPM: r_e = r_f + β × market risk premium. With a 3% risk-free rate, a beta of 1.2 and a 5% market risk premium, r_e = 3% + 1.2 × 5% = 9%. Unlike debt, equity has no stated interest rate, so its cost is inferred from the return investors require for the risk they bear.",

  aboutCapitalStructureIntro: "Modigliani and Miller showed that, without taxes and frictions, a firm's value does not depend on how it is financed. This tool shows how the cost of equity rises with leverage, how WACC stays flat without taxes, and how the interest tax shield lowers WACC and adds value once corporate taxes are introduced.",
  aboutCapitalStructureQ1: "Why does the cost of equity rise with leverage?",
  aboutCapitalStructureA1: "More debt makes the remaining equity riskier, because shareholders bear the business risk on a smaller base. Their required return rises exactly enough to keep WACC unchanged in the no-tax case.",
  aboutCapitalStructureQ2: "What is the interest tax shield?",
  aboutCapitalStructureA2: "The tax saving from deducting interest payments. With corporate taxes it makes debt financing cheaper and adds its present value to the firm.",
  aboutCapitalStructureQ3: "What are Modigliani–Miller propositions I and II?",
  aboutCapitalStructureA3: "Proposition I says that without taxes, bankruptcy costs or other frictions, a firm's value does not depend on its mix of debt and equity. Proposition II says the cost of equity rises linearly with leverage: r_e = r_A + (D/E)(r_A − r_d), where r_A is the return on the firm's assets. Together they mean cheaper debt is exactly offset by more expensive equity.",
  aboutCapitalStructureQ4: "Is there an optimal capital structure?",
  aboutCapitalStructureA4: "With corporate taxes alone, more debt always adds value through the tax shield. In practice the expected costs of financial distress and bankruptcy grow with leverage, so the trade-off theory places the optimum where the marginal tax benefit of debt equals its marginal expected distress cost.",

  aboutRandomWalkIntro: "The random walk is the discrete starting point of stochastic models: add up independent ±1 coin-flip steps and watch the sum wander. This lab simulates seeded walks, rescales them by √n towards Brownian motion, measures how the spread grows with time and shows the central limit theorem turning sums of any steps into a normal distribution.",
  aboutRandomWalkQ1: "What is a symmetric random walk?",
  aboutRandomWalkA1: "A sum of independent steps that are +1 or −1 with equal probability. Its expected value stays 0, but its variance grows by 1 every step, so after n steps the typical distance from the start is √n.",
  aboutRandomWalkQ2: "Why does the spread grow like √t and not like t?",
  aboutRandomWalkA2: "Independent steps partly cancel. Variances of independent steps add, so variance grows linearly in time and the standard deviation, its square root, grows like √t.",
  aboutRandomWalkQ3: "How does a random walk become Brownian motion?",
  aboutRandomWalkA3: "Shrink the step size to 1/√n while taking n steps per unit of time. As n grows, the rescaled walk converges to Brownian motion (Donsker's principle); the Brownian Motion lab picks up from there.",
  aboutRandomWalkQ4: "What does the central limit theorem say?",
  aboutRandomWalkA4: "The standardised sum of many independent steps with finite variance is approximately normal, whatever the distribution of a single step. That is why the endpoint of a long walk looks Gaussian.",

  aboutBrownianMotionIntro: "Brownian motion is the continuous-time random walk at the heart of financial models. This simulator draws arithmetic Brownian motion with a chosen drift and volatility, using reproducible seeded paths, so you can see how the drift sets the trend and the volatility sets the spread that grows over time.",
  aboutBrownianMotionQ1: "What is the difference between drift and volatility?",
  aboutBrownianMotionA1: "Drift μ is the average change per unit time, the slope of the mean path. Volatility σ scales the random part; the spread around the mean grows like σ√t.",
  aboutBrownianMotionQ2: "Can arithmetic Brownian motion become negative?",
  aboutBrownianMotionA2: "Yes. Its value is normally distributed, so it can take any real value. That is why stock prices are usually modelled with geometric Brownian motion instead.",
  aboutBrownianMotionQ3: "What is Brownian motion in finance?",
  aboutBrownianMotionA3: "A continuous-time random process whose changes over non-overlapping intervals are independent and normally distributed, with a variance proportional to the length of the interval. It models the unpredictable part of prices and interest rates; arithmetic Brownian motion X_t = X₀ + μt + σW_t adds a constant drift to it.",
  aboutBrownianMotionQ4: "Why are Brownian paths so jagged?",
  aboutBrownianMotionA4: "Over a short interval Δt a typical move has size σ√Δt, which is much larger than Δt itself. Zooming in therefore never makes the path look straight: Brownian motion is continuous but nowhere differentiable.",

  aboutGbmIntro: "Geometric Brownian motion is the standard model for stock prices and the foundation of the Black–Scholes model. Prices change by random percentages rather than random amounts, so they stay positive and are lognormally distributed. This simulator shows sample paths for a given drift and volatility together with the theoretical expected value S₀e^{μt}.",
  aboutGbmQ1: "Why is GBM used for stock prices?",
  aboutGbmA1: "It keeps prices positive and makes returns, not price changes, the random quantity, matching the idea that a stock is as likely to rise 10% whether it trades at 10 or 100.",
  aboutGbmQ2: "Why is the typical path below the expected value?",
  aboutGbmA2: "The lognormal distribution is skewed: a few very high outcomes pull the mean up, while the median path grows only at μ − ½σ².",
  aboutGbmQ3: "What is the formula for geometric Brownian motion?",
  aboutGbmA3: "The price follows dS = μS dt + σS dW, whose solution is S_t = S₀ exp((μ − ½σ²)t + σW_t). The log price is therefore normally distributed with mean ln S₀ + (μ − ½σ²)t and variance σ²t, and the price itself is lognormal.",
  aboutGbmQ4: "What are the drawbacks of GBM as a stock price model?",
  aboutGbmA4: "It assumes constant volatility and normally distributed log returns. Real returns have fatter tails, sudden jumps and volatility that changes over time and rises in sell-offs, which is why stochastic volatility models such as Heston and jump-diffusion models extend it.",

  aboutItoProcessIntro: "Itô's lemma is the chain rule of stochastic calculus. Applied to the logarithm of geometric Brownian motion, it shows that log prices drift at μ − ½σ², not μ. This visualization makes that −½σ² correction visible and explains where it comes from: the quadratic variation of Brownian motion.",
  aboutItoProcessQ1: "Why does Itô's lemma have an extra term?",
  aboutItoProcessA1: "Brownian increments are of size √dt, so (dW)² behaves like dt and does not vanish. The second-order Taylor term ½f″σ²dt therefore survives, unlike in ordinary calculus.",
  aboutItoProcessQ2: "What is the −½σ² term in practice?",
  aboutItoProcessA2: "It is the gap between the arithmetic mean return μ and the growth rate of a typical path. Higher volatility lowers the long-run growth of a single investment even when the mean return is unchanged.",
  aboutItoProcessQ3: "What is an Itô process?",
  aboutItoProcessA3: "A process of the form dX = a(t, X) dt + b(t, X) dW: a drift term plus a term driven by Brownian motion. Brownian motion with drift, geometric Brownian motion and mean-reverting interest rate models are all Itô processes, and Itô's lemma tells you how any smooth function of one evolves.",
  aboutItoProcessQ4: "How do you apply Itô's lemma to ln S?",
  aboutItoProcessA4: "For f(S) = ln S, f′ = 1/S and f″ = −1/S². With dS = μS dt + σS dW, Itô's lemma gives d(ln S) = (μ − ½σ²) dt + σ dW, so the log price is a Brownian motion with drift μ − ½σ².",

  aboutMonteCarloIntro: "Monte Carlo simulation prices an option by averaging its discounted payoff over many simulated outcomes. This tool simulates terminal stock prices under risk-neutral geometric Brownian motion and shows the estimate and its confidence interval converging towards the Black–Scholes price as the number of paths grows.",
  aboutMonteCarloQ1: "How fast does a Monte Carlo estimate converge?",
  aboutMonteCarloA1: "The standard error falls like 1/√N: four times as many simulations halve the error.",
  aboutMonteCarloQ2: "Why use Monte Carlo if Black–Scholes has a formula?",
  aboutMonteCarloA2: "Here the formula is the benchmark that shows the method works. Monte Carlo becomes essential for path-dependent payoffs, many underlyings and models without closed-form prices.",
  aboutMonteCarloQ3: "How do you price an option with Monte Carlo simulation?",
  aboutMonteCarloA3: "Simulate many terminal stock prices under the risk-neutral measure, S_T = S₀ exp((r − ½σ²)T + σ√T Z) with Z standard normal, compute the payoff for each, average the payoffs and discount the average at the risk-free rate. The estimate converges to the true price as the number of simulations grows.",
  aboutMonteCarloQ4: "What is the confidence interval of a Monte Carlo price?",
  aboutMonteCarloA4: "The range estimate ± 1.96 × standard error, where the standard error is the sample standard deviation of the discounted payoffs divided by √N. About 95% of such intervals contain the true price, so the interval tells you how many simulations a given accuracy needs.",

  aboutGuideIntro: "Narrative guides that connect the interactive tools into a story: financial instruments and their payoffs, the no-arbitrage principle, risk-neutral pricing, delta hedging and the road to the Black–Scholes formula. Each guide links to the labs where you can try the ideas yourself.",
  aboutGuideQ1: "In what order should I read the guides?",
  aboutGuideA1: "Start with instruments and no-arbitrage, then risk-neutral pricing and delta hedging; together they lead to the Black–Scholes formula.",
  aboutGuideQ2: "Who are the guides for?",
  aboutGuideA2: "Students of financial and insurance mathematics and anyone who wants intuition behind derivatives pricing, not just the formulas.",
  aboutImmunizationIntro: "Immunization funds a known future liability with bonds so that interest-rate moves do not open a funding gap. Match the present value and the duration of the assets to the liability, and the surplus barely reacts to a parallel shift in yields. Over the years the match drifts as time passes and the curve moves, so you rebalance, at a cost. This lab plays that as a game: each round brings a seeded rate shock, and you see why the surplus moved.",
  aboutImmunizationQ1: "What is immunization?",
  aboutImmunizationA1: "A strategy that holds assets whose present value and duration equal those of a liability. A small parallel move in yields then changes assets and liability by about the same amount, so the surplus stays near zero.",
  aboutImmunizationQ2: "Why does the portfolio need rebalancing?",
  aboutImmunizationA2: "Duration changes with time and with yields, and the liability's duration shrinks by a year every year while a bond's duration shrinks more slowly. The match therefore drifts, and the portfolio has to be reset to stay immunized.",
  aboutImmunizationQ3: "What does convexity add?",
  aboutImmunizationA3: "Duration matching covers small moves. For larger moves the second-order term matters: assets with more convexity than the liability gain more when yields fall than they lose when yields rise, so the surplus tends to be positive after large shifts.",
  aboutImmunizationQ4: "What is a twist, and why does it hurt?",
  aboutImmunizationA4: "A twist moves long and short yields in opposite directions. Duration matching assumes a parallel shift, so a twist can leave a gap even in a perfectly matched portfolio.",

  aboutOrderBookIntro: "A limit order book is the list of all resting buy and sell orders for an asset, sorted by price. Buyers post bids below the market, sellers post asks above it, and the gap between the best bid and the best ask is the spread. Orders at the same price are filled in the order they arrived, which is called price–time priority. A market order trades immediately against the best resting orders and, if it is larger than the quantity at the best price, keeps eating into worse prices. This lab lets you build that picture by hand: send orders, watch the book react and see what each trade really cost you.",
  aboutOrderBookQ1: "What is price–time priority?",
  aboutOrderBookA1: "It is the rule that decides who trades first. Orders with a better price always come first: the highest bid and the lowest ask. Among orders at the same price, the one that arrived earliest is filled first. That is why a trader who joins a busy price level late can wait a long time, or never be filled at all, even though their price is as good as everyone else's in that queue.",
  aboutOrderBookQ2: "What does it mean that a market order walks the book?",
  aboutOrderBookA2: "A market order takes whatever liquidity is available, best price first. If you buy 500 shares and only 200 are offered at the best ask, the next 300 are bought at the next price levels up. Your average price is therefore worse than the best ask, and the larger the order relative to the depth of the book, the further it walks and the worse the average becomes.",
  aboutOrderBookQ3: "How is slippage measured?",
  aboutOrderBookA3: "Slippage compares what you actually paid with a benchmark, usually the mid price before you traded: slippage = average fill price − mid for a buy, and mid − average fill price for a sell. Half the spread is the minimum you pay on any market order; anything beyond that comes from walking the book. In the lab, try the same order size on a deep book and on a thin one and compare the two numbers.",
  aboutOrderBookQ4: "Is a limit order always cheaper than a market order?",
  aboutOrderBookA4: "Not necessarily. A limit order avoids paying the spread, but it may never be filled, and it tends to be filled exactly when the price is moving against you, because someone better informed chose to trade with it. The real comparison is the expected cost including the risk of missing the trade, which the queue part of the lab lets you explore.",

  aboutMarketMakingIntro: "A market maker stands ready to buy at the bid and sell at the ask, all day, to anyone. When a buyer and a seller arrive one after the other, the market maker earns the spread between the two prices. That is the business: income is roughly the spread times the volume that is matched on both sides. The catch is that customers do not arrive in neat pairs. Between trades the market maker holds inventory whose value moves with the market, and some customers trade because they know where the price is going. This lab lets you play the role yourself and see where the money comes from and where it goes.",
  aboutMarketMakingQ1: "How does a market maker make money?",
  aboutMarketMakingA1: "By buying at the bid and selling at the ask. Each matched round trip earns the spread, so a first estimate of income is spread × matched volume. A wider spread earns more per trade but attracts fewer customers, because they can find better prices elsewhere or simply decide not to trade. The best spread balances income per trade against the number of trades, which you can find by experiment in the lab.",
  aboutMarketMakingQ2: "Why does a wider spread not always mean more profit?",
  aboutMarketMakingA2: "Because the number of fills falls as the spread widens. If the arrival rate of customers decays with the distance of your quote from the fair price, doubling the spread can more than halve the volume. Total income is the product of the two, so it rises at first and then falls. A spread that is too narrow, on the other hand, may not cover the losses to informed traders.",
  aboutMarketMakingQ3: "What is inventory risk?",
  aboutMarketMakingA3: "When more customers sell to you than buy from you, you end up long the asset, and the other way round. That position gains or loses as the price moves, often much more than the spread you earned on the trades. Market makers therefore shade their quotes: with a long position they lower both bid and ask to encourage buyers and discourage further sellers, until the inventory comes back towards zero.",
  aboutMarketMakingQ4: "What is adverse selection?",
  aboutMarketMakingA4: "Some customers trade because they know the price is about to move. They buy from you just before the price rises and sell to you just before it falls, so every trade with them loses money. A common misconception is that the spread is pure profit; in reality part of it is an insurance premium paid by uninformed traders to cover the losses to informed ones.",

  aboutAvellanedaStoikovIntro: "The Avellaneda–Stoikov model answers a precise question: where should a risk-averse market maker place the bid and the ask? The answer has two parts. First, the centre of the quotes is not the mid price but a reservation price that moves away from it in proportion to the inventory: r = s − qγσ²(T − t), where q is the position, γ the risk aversion, σ the volatility and T − t the time left. Second, the total spread is γσ²(T − t) + (2/γ)·ln(1 + γ/k), where k says how fast order arrivals fall off with distance. This lab turns both formulas into something you can see move.",
  aboutAvellanedaStoikovQ1: "What is the reservation price?",
  aboutAvellanedaStoikovA1: "It is the price at which the market maker is indifferent between holding the current inventory and not holding it. With a long position the reservation price lies below the mid, because the maker would accept a little less to get rid of risk. Quoting around it instead of around the mid skews both quotes: with a long inventory the ask becomes more attractive and the bid less so, and the position tends to shrink.",
  aboutAvellanedaStoikovQ2: "Where does the optimal spread come from?",
  aboutAvellanedaStoikovA2: "It has two terms. The term γσ²(T − t) is compensation for the risk of holding inventory until the end of the horizon. The term (2/γ)·ln(1 + γ/k) comes from the order arrival: when arrivals fall off quickly with distance (large k), quoting wide loses too many trades, so the spread is tighter. A very risk-averse maker quotes wider to trade less often.",
  aboutAvellanedaStoikovQ3: "What should I try in the lab?",
  aboutAvellanedaStoikovA3: "Start with zero inventory and see the quotes sit symmetrically around the mid. Then push the inventory positive and watch both quotes slide down. Raise γ and the skew grows; shorten the time left and the skew fades because there is less time for the price to move. Finally draw your own policy and compare its P&L and inventory path with the model's on the same seed.",
  aboutAvellanedaStoikovQ4: "Is a higher risk aversion always safer?",
  aboutAvellanedaStoikovA4: "It reduces inventory swings, but it is not free. A very large γ pushes the quotes wide and skews them hard, so the maker trades less and gives away edge on almost every fill it does get. The common misconception is that zero inventory is the goal; the model actually trades a little inventory risk for spread income, and the best γ depends on how you score the result.",

  aboutGlostenMilgromIntro: "The Glosten–Milgrom model explains the bid-ask spread with information alone. The asset is worth either a high or a low value, and the market maker does not know which. Each arriving trader is informed with probability π and knows the true value, or is a noise trader who buys or sells at random. A buy is therefore a hint that the value is high. A competitive market maker who breaks even on average sets the ask equal to the expected value given a buy, ask = E[V | buy], and the bid equal to the expected value given a sell, bid = E[V | sell]. The spread is the gap between those two conditional expectations.",
  aboutGlostenMilgromQ1: "Why is the ask above the bid if there are no costs?",
  aboutGlostenMilgromA1: "Because a buy order carries information. If some buyers know the value is high, the market maker's best guess of the value after seeing a buy is higher than after seeing a sell. Setting each quote to that conditional expectation means the maker breaks even against the mix of traders, and the gap between the two is the spread. More informed traders, a larger π, means a wider spread.",
  aboutGlostenMilgromQ2: "How does the market maker learn?",
  aboutGlostenMilgromA2: "By Bayes' rule. After every trade the maker updates the probability that the value is high: a buy raises it and a sell lowers it, by an amount that depends on π. Over many trades the belief drifts towards the truth, the quotes follow it and the spread narrows as uncertainty shrinks. The lab draws this belief path trade by trade so you can see the learning happen.",
  aboutGlostenMilgromQ3: "Are the market maker's beliefs a martingale?",
  aboutGlostenMilgromA3: "Yes. Given everything seen so far, the expected next belief equals the current belief, so the quoted mid has no predictable drift. Beliefs do move, sometimes a lot, but never in a direction the maker could have forecast. This is the same idea as an efficient price: the price already reflects all public information, and only new trades move it.",
  aboutGlostenMilgromQ4: "Who pays for the informed traders' profits?",
  aboutGlostenMilgromA4: "The noise traders. Against informed traders the maker loses on every trade, against noise traders it gains the half-spread, and the quotes are set so the two cancel on average. A common misconception is that the market maker profits from the spread here; in this model its expected profit is zero, and the spread is a transfer from uninformed to informed traders.",

  aboutKyleIntro: "In the Kyle model one insider knows the true value of an asset, noise traders submit random orders, and a market maker sees only the total order flow. The maker cannot tell the insider's orders from the noise, so it sets the price as a linear function of the flow: Δp = λ × order flow, where λ, Kyle's lambda, measures price impact. The insider wants to profit from the information but knows that trading aggressively moves the price against them. The optimal strategy trades gradually and hides in the noise, so that the information leaks into the price at a steady pace. This lab puts you in the insider's seat.",
  aboutKyleQ1: "What is Kyle's lambda?",
  aboutKyleA1: "It is the price impact per unit of order flow: if the net order flow is x, the price moves by λx. A large λ means a thin, illiquid market where every order moves the price a lot; a small λ means a deep market. In the model λ grows with the uncertainty about the value and falls with the amount of noise trading, because more noise gives the market maker less reason to read flow as information.",
  aboutKyleQ2: "Why does the insider not trade everything at once?",
  aboutKyleA2: "Because a large order would be read as information and move the price to the true value immediately, leaving almost no profit. By spreading trades out, the insider keeps each round's order small relative to the noise, so the price adjusts only partly each time. In the continuous version the insider trades so that the price converges to the true value exactly at the end.",
  aboutKyleQ3: "Who loses money to the insider?",
  aboutKyleA3: "The noise traders. The market maker sets prices so that it breaks even on average, so the insider's expected profit comes entirely from the noise traders, who buy and sell for reasons unrelated to the value. More noise trading means more camouflage and a larger profit for the insider, but also a smaller loss per noise trade.",
  aboutKyleQ4: "What should I try in the lab?",
  aboutKyleA4: "Play a few rounds as the insider and compare your profit with the optimal strategy on the same seed. Then try trading all at once and see the price jump to the value. Finally estimate λ by drawing a line through the scatter of order flow against price change. A common misconception is that price impact is a cost of trading large orders only; here it is how information gets into prices.",

  aboutSpreadEstimationIntro: "Quotes are not always available, but trade prices usually are. Spread estimators recover the bid-ask spread from the trades themselves. Roll's measure uses the fact that trades bounce between the bid and the ask: buys print high and sells print low, so consecutive price changes are negatively correlated. If the true value follows a random walk, the spread is s = 2√(−cov(Δp_t, Δp_{t−1})). When quotes are available, the effective spread measures what each trade really cost: 2|p − mid|, twice the distance of the trade price from the mid. This lab lets you classify a tape by hand and compare both measures.",
  aboutSpreadEstimationQ1: "Why are consecutive price changes negatively correlated?",
  aboutSpreadEstimationA1: "Because of bid-ask bounce. If the last trade was a buy at the ask, the next trade is equally likely to be a buy or a sell, so on average the next price change is downward. After a sell at the bid it is upward on average. Even when the true value does not move at all, the trade price zigzags between the two quotes and the changes have negative autocovariance.",
  aboutSpreadEstimationQ2: "How does the Roll measure work?",
  aboutSpreadEstimationA2: "With a constant half-spread c and random buy/sell directions, the covariance of consecutive price changes is −c². Solving for the spread gives s = 2c = 2√(−cov). You only need a series of trade prices. If the sample covariance happens to be positive, the formula has no real answer; that is common in short samples and a sign the estimate is unreliable.",
  aboutSpreadEstimationQ3: "What is the effective spread?",
  aboutSpreadEstimationA3: "It measures the cost of a single trade against the mid quote at the time: effective spread = 2 × |trade price − mid|. A trade at the ask has an effective spread equal to the quoted spread; a trade inside the quotes has a smaller one. Averaging over many trades gives the cost that traders actually paid, which can differ from the quoted spread on the screen.",
  aboutSpreadEstimationQ4: "Why does informed trading make Roll's estimate too low?",
  aboutSpreadEstimationA4: "Roll assumes buys and sells are independent of future price moves. Informed traders break that: after a buy the value tends to rise, which adds positive correlation to the price changes and cancels part of the negative bounce. The covariance becomes less negative and Roll's estimate falls below the true spread. The lab lets you raise the informed share and watch the gap open.",

  aboutSystemicRiskIntro: "Systemic risk is the danger that trouble at one financial institution spreads through the system and damages many others, not only the one that failed. Banks, funds and clearing houses lend to each other, trade derivatives with each other and hold many of the same assets, so the financial system is really a network. This tool lets you draw that network, rank institutions by how much damage their failure would cause, and watch a shock travel through it. Every institution is a stylised archetype, such as a dealer bank or a money market fund, not a real firm.",
  aboutSystemicRiskQ1: "What is systemic risk?",
  aboutSystemicRiskA1: "It is the risk of a chain reaction: one institution's distress forces losses, asset sales or withdrawals on others until a large part of the system is impaired. It differs from ordinary risk because the damage does not come only from the first failure but from how institutions are connected. A small bank can be harmless on its own yet dangerous if it sits at the centre of many obligations, which is why regulators study the network and not just each balance sheet.",
  aboutSystemicRiskQ2: "How does a shock spread through a financial network?",
  aboutSystemicRiskA2: "Through three main channels. Counterparty losses: when a borrower defaults, its lenders write off part of what they were owed. Fire sales: a stressed institution sells assets to cut leverage, prices fall, and everyone holding the same assets loses value. Funding runs: short-term lenders sense weakness, refuse to roll over and withdraw cash, forcing yet more sales. The channels reinforce each other, so contagion can be far larger than any single link suggests.",
  aboutSystemicRiskQ3: "Why is a financial network called robust yet fragile?",
  aboutSystemicRiskA3: "Dense links spread a small shock across many balance sheets, so each absorbs only a little and the system survives. Beyond a certain size the same links carry the shock to everyone at once and turn a few failures into many. Connectivity therefore helps against small shocks and hurts against large ones. The shock simulation lets you sweep the size of the first loss and find where a network flips from absorbing the blow to amplifying it.",
  aboutSystemicRiskQ4: "Are these real banks?",
  aboutSystemicRiskA4: "No. Every institution here is a stylised archetype with round-number balance sheets, such as a dealer bank, a money market fund or a clearing house. The historical episodes use real dates and public events as context, but no figures are attached to real firms. The aim is to show mechanisms and compare network shapes, not to estimate how any actual institution would fare, so do not read the numbers as predictions.",
};
